<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dost Mohammad Khan of Bhopal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1672–1728</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> was an Afghan soldier from Tirah who served in the Mughal army and, as imperial authority weakened after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s death in 1707, carved out his own state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. He built a fort at Islamnagar, developed Bhopal into his capital and became the first nawab of Bhopal. His dynasty ruled Bhopal State until 1949, including a century under four successive women, the Begums of Bhopal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan-of-bhopal.jpg" alt="Dost Mohammad Khan of Bhopal" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dost_Mohammad_Khan,_Nawab_of_Bhopal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
